# Frozen: code-file HEADER pointers that name a docs page and no section.
# Two-way: a row whose file stops carrying that bare pointer -- because it gained an
# anchor, or moved -- is STALE and the line goes. The key is <file>\t<page>, never a
# line number.
# docs/code-quality-tooling.md#header-pointers-must-name-a-section

# The page IS the subject. Naming it names the section: these pages exist for exactly
# the one file that points at them, so an anchor would add a second name for the same
# thing and rot on the next heading rewrite. Not debt.
docs/scripts/verify_doc_dupes.py	docs/INDEX.md
linux/scripts/01-core/resource-monitor.sh	docs/build-resource-monitoring.md
linux/scripts/lib/slang-compile.sh	docs/slang-shader-compilation.md
linux/scripts/tests/test-artifact-copy-completeness.sh	docs/artifact-copy-completeness.md
linux/scripts/tests/test-genai-smoke-payload.sh	docs/gen1-riscv64-genai.md
linux/scripts/tests/test-litert-qairt-guard.sh	docs/qnn-linux.md
linux/scripts/tests/test-riscv64-isa-gate.sh	docs/riscv64-rva23-baseline.md

# Same reason, one step stronger: the page is this script's OUTPUT. code-quality-gates.md
# is generated by verify_gate_registry.py --write and carries a single heading, so an
# anchor there could only ever repeat the page name. The line below it in that docstring
# already points at the prose that explains the gate, and that one IS anchored.
linux/scripts/verify_gate_registry.py	docs/code-quality-gates.md
